    "q-80800": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80800",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
